SELinux is completely disabled at the kernel level on this Ubuntu 24.04 system (no /sys/fs/selinux mount, no policy loaded). Ubuntu uses AppArmor by default. The SELinux userspace tools (selinux-utils, policycoreutils) were installed, and /etc/selinux/config exists with SELINUX=permissive, but the running kernel does not support SELinux.

2. Switch SELinux to Enforcing mode using setenforce and verify the change with getenforce.<br><br><em><strong>Hint:</strong> You may need sudo privileges to change the mode.</em>
