The mode could not be changed because SELinux is disabled in the kernel. setenforce only works when SELinux is already active (Enforcing or Permissive). Enabling full SELinux on Ubuntu requires a SELinux-enabled kernel, policy packages (selinux-policy-default), and a reboot — none of which are feasible in this sandbox environment.

