Ran getenforce and sestatus → confirmed Disabled.
Attempted setenforce 1 → failed with “SELinux is disabled”.
Created /tmp/testapp with a test file.
Attempted chcon -t user_home_t and chcon -t httpd_sys_content_t → both failed (unlabeled files, no SELinux kernel support).
Checked for audit logs / AVC denials → none present.

Why SELinux contexts matter (general explanation):

SELinux uses security contexts (user:role:type:level) on processes and files. A process such as httpd_t is only allowed to read files labeled httpd_sys_content_t (or similar allowed types). If a file is labeled user_home_t, the kernel denies access even if traditional Unix permissions (rwx) would allow it. The denial is logged as an AVC (Access Vector Cache) message. Changing the label with chcon (temporary) or semanage fcontext + restorecon (persistent) restores access without weakening the overall policy.
Conclusion for this environment:

SELinux is Disabled and cannot be activated or tested for access denials. The commands and concepts above are the correct ones for a real SELinux-enabled system (e.g., RHEL, Fedora, or a properly configured Ubuntu with SELinux kernel). On a working SELinux system the sequence would be:

setenforce 1
Create directory → chcon -t user_home_t ... → observe AVC denial in audit log when Apache tries to serve the file
chcon -t httpd_sys_content_t ... → access succeeds
